fact WorkItem(id: "WI-955", description: some(value: "reconstruct_sort_params' two paths disagree on a NESTED SortAlias, and WI-402 already produces one.\n\n`reconstruct_sort_params` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs) recovers a sort's type params from its SortAlias facts by TWO routes that are supposed to agree:\n  * INDEXED (`SortAliasIndex.by_parent`, built by `build_sort_alias_index`): keys the alias's qualified name with `rsplit_once('.')` — LAST segment is the param, the rest is the parent.\n  * PRE-INDEX SCAN (load-time value typing, when `kb.sort_alias_index` is None): keeps every alias whose name has the parent's as a `.`-boundary PREFIX, then slices EVERYTHING after it as the param name.\n\nThey agree only for a one-segment param. `build_sort_alias_index`'s own comment states the premise: 'sorts don't nest, so no `Sort.Inner.T` alias exists to make the last-segment split and the prefix test disagree.' THAT PREMISE IS FALSE TODAY.\n\nDRIVEN (WI-943 /simplify altitude pass, probe run against current main):\n\n  sort Holder\n    sort T = ?\n    entity holder(item: T)\n    operation mk() -> C ensures KVStore[C, K = String, V = Int64]\n  end\n\n  SortAlias sources under `<ns>.Holder.` = [\"T\", \"mk.C\"]\n  type_params_of_sort(Holder)            = [\"T\"]\n\nWI-402's existential carrier (`build_existential_return`, kb/load.rs ~14935) defines `<op_qualified>.<carrier>` and calls `emit_type_param_backing_var` on it — a SortAlias named `<ns>.<Sort>.<op>.C`. The pre-index scan slices `mk.C` as a PARAM NAME of `Holder` and (per its WI-384 tail) injects it as a `mk.C = ?_` binding into every constructor type it reconstructs for `Holder`; the indexed path files the same alias under the OPERATION and a lookup for the SORT never sees it. Silent drop on one path, garbage param on the other.\n\nNOT DRIVEN, and the ticket should start here: I measured that the SHAPE reaches the slicing rule and that the two keying rules disagree on it. I did NOT drive a user-visible wrong outcome — the pre-index path runs only in the load-time value-typing window, and no test moved. FIRST QUESTION: is there a program where a constructor of a sort with an existential-returning member gets the spurious binding into a type that is then compared? If not, this is a latent trap to close, not a live bug — say which, in the ticket, before fixing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ITSELF: this is the exact hazard that made WI-943 REFUSE the obvious unification (assert a `SortAlias` per operation bracket parameter, so both kinds publish through one channel). That alternative would take this rare shape and make it universal — 45 `operation X[…]` brackets in stdlib alone. Closing this reopens that design option; see the loader-published-index sibling ticket.\n\nSCOPE: make the two keyings ONE. The prefix test cannot distinguish `Sort.param` from `Sort.op.param` from name alone — the symbol table can (`declaring_scope_symbol`), and `type_params_of_sort` already gives the declared list the reconstruction should be driven BY rather than re-derived from names.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven — a test that the two paths return the SAME params for a sort with an existential-returning member (force each path: run the reconstruction before and after `build_sort_alias_index`), and that neither reports a param `type_params_of_sort` does not list. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-943 /simplify altitude review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T11:52:42Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-955", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Answer to the ticket's FIRST QUESTION: LATENT TRAP, not a live bug.\n\nMEASURED (the decisive control): with the pre-index branch of reconstruct_sort_params replaced by a panic!, the ENTIRE workspace suite passed - 4069 tests, 0 failures, 0 runtime panics. Nothing in the tested surface reaches that path. It is reachable in principle - value_type_term's callers are resolve_bridge_requirements, value_carrier_overrides_eq_family_deep, simp_requires_guard_holds, find_dictionary_guard and typed_pattern_bounds_hold, any of which can run during load (a [simp] requires guard, a typed pattern bound, a dictionary guard under a compile-time macro / const) before type_check_sorts_collect builds the index - but no program in the repo drives it.\n\nTHE DISAGREEMENT ITSELF, driven before the fix:\n  reconstruct_sort_params(Holder)  indexed=[\"T\"]  scanned=[\"T\",\"mk.C\"]  type_params_of_sort=[\"T\"]\n  value_type_term(holder(item: 42))  indexed Holder[T = Int64]  scanned Holder[T = Int64, mk.C = ?_]\n\nCORRECTION to the ticket's own framing: it says 'Silent drop on one path, garbage param on the other.' The INDEX was not dropping anything - filing <ns>.Holder.mk.C under mk IS the right answer, because Holder must not see it. Only the scan was wrong. Verified: for all 106 var-target SortAlias facts in a stdlib load the last-segment split and the symbol-table scope link agree exactly (0 disagreements, 0 with no readable scope), so the index half of this fix is behaviour-identical by construction.\n\nFIX - one rule, and one gate. sort_param_alias_entry(kb, alias_functor, target) is now the sole answer to 'is this alias a sort's declared type parameter, whose, and under what name'; build_sort_alias_index's by_parent and reconstruct_sort_params' pre-index scan are the same two calls in the same order. by_parent re-keyed String -> Symbol. reconstruct_sort_params split into sort_param_alias_vars (index-or-scan) + ONE shared WI-384/WI-516 tail (it was duplicated once per branch).\n\nWENT DEEPER THAN THE TICKET ASKED, on evidence: the gate is is_sort_param_symbol, which already existed and already documented the exact distinction ('sort T = ? inside sort Stream { … } IS a type-param; sort Term = ? at namespace level is a top-level abstract sort, not a type parameter'). Without it the reader answers 'declared in the scope of', not 'a declared type parameter of', and by_parent files 9 top-level abstract sorts under NAMESPACE parents (reflect's Term/Symbol/FactRef/SourceId/NodeOccurrence/ConstraintId, prelude.Type/Unit, kernel.T) - inert only because no caller passes a namespace symbol, a caller accident rather than a property. MEASURED with the gate: 55 parents kept, exactly those 9 dropped, and the entries recovered for a parent equal type_params_of_sort for EVERY parent - 0 mismatches. The ticket's acceptance ('neither reports a param type_params_of_sort does not list') is now true by construction, not by caller accident.\n\nDEVIATION from SCOPE, stated. The ticket said the reconstruction 'should be driven BY' type_params_of_sort. It is not, and the real competitor is not type_params_of_sort but sort_type_params_as_pairs (typing.rs), which IS driven by the declared list and IS memoized. Three verified blockers, now written at sort_param_alias_vars: (a) its sort_param_pairs_cache has a get and an insert and NO invalidator, so populating it from the load-time window would let a half-loaded sort's param list poison every type-check-time reader; (b) it resolves each param through resolve_sort_alias, whose last rung is the name-directed sort_alias_by_name guess this reader just stopped making; (c) it keys pairs by the QUALIFIED param symbol, not the bare one make_parameterized_type needs. type_params_of_sort alone also cannot serve: it returns NAMES with no backing VarId, and it scans symbols.by_qualified_name in full on a per-constructor-node path. It is the tests' ORACLE instead.\n\nALSO, at the user's direction during review: all three readers of the SortAlias relation - build_sort_alias_index, scan_sort_aliases and sort_param_alias_vars - now decode a fact through one carrier-neutral sort_alias_head_slots (rule_head_value + TermView, the surface load's sort_alias_exists already uses), returning (source functor Symbol, target TermId) - the shapes the consumers actually use - with the is_fact test folded in so a caller cannot forget it. fact_head_term answers None for a Value::Node-carried head and skipped the fact WHOLE on a property no consumer depends on. Not a behaviour change: measured 0 value-carried heads among 107 SortAlias facts, and for a loader-produced alias a value-carried head and a non-term target are the same condition. Routing scan_sort_aliases was found by BOTH the /simplify reuse and altitude passes - my first cut had converted only two of the three, which would have introduced this ticket's own defect class one map over (index vs its own pre-index fallback disagreeing about which facts exist).\n\nTESTS - wi955_one_alias_keying_tests (typing.rs):\n  both_paths_report_the_declared_params_and_nothing_else\n  the_constructor_type_is_identical_before_and_after_the_index\nCONTROL, measured on the final code by restoring the prefix slice: they fail with [\"T\",\"mk.C\"] vs [\"T\"], and Holder[T = Int64] vs Holder[T = Int64, mk.C = ?_]. The INDEXED arm passes either way BY DESIGN (the split already reached the right answer) and is asserted so that a re-keying which merely LOSES params fails loudly instead of letting both paths agree at zero. The type assertion renders BINDINGS, not just keys - a keys-only check would pass on Holder[T] carrying no Int64 at all.\n\ncargo-test: 4071 passed, 0 failed (baseline 4069 + these 2).\n\nREOPENS the WI-943 alternative's keying hazard only: a SortAlias per operation bracket param is declared in the OPERATION's scope, so it files under the operation and can never be read as a param of an enclosing sort. WI-954 still refuses that alternative for its other reasons (pollutes by_name, redefines a user-queryable relation).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-960 (make_type_var per-call symbol resolution on the constructor-typing hot path), WI-958 (three spellings of 'operation -> its parent sort'), WI-959 (two remaining hand-rolled stdlib fixtures in typing.rs test modules). Feedback added to WI-954 and WI-956.", at: "2026-08-03T11:53:20Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-955", author: "user", content: "ADDENDUM (user review, after delivery). USER ASKED: 'why do you think that type-param aliases of parent can be only var?'\n\nMy comment justified the Var::Global gate with 'a sort T = Int64 alias resolves to a sort ref and pins nothing' - an argument about the TARGET, and the weak version. The real guarantee is upstream in the REGISTRATION: add_type_param is gated on matches!(s.definition, TypeExpr::Variable { .. }) (load.rs, Item::AbstractSort arm of scan_items_pass1), so an in-body 'sort T = Int64' or 'sort U = List[T]' is never a declared type parameter at all - it is not in type_params_of_sort, and with is_sort_param_symbol as the membership gate it never reaches the target check. Every form that IS a param and carries a SortAlias gets it from emit_type_param_backing_var (WI-452 marked, WI-402 carrier) or from load_abstract_sort's Variable arm - a Var::Global in each case. An operation's BRACKET params publish through OperationInfo.type_params and produce no SortAlias.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, fixed: Var::Global is not a filter here, it is the EXTRACTION of the var the reconstruction reads out of the substitution, and it cannot fail for a declared param. Silently returning None on it would make the sort quietly lose a param - the arity-shrinking WI-384 exists to prevent - so it is now a debug_assert! naming the alias and the target found. Measured vacuous: across 55 parents the recovered entries equal type_params_of_sort exactly, which is only possible if no declared param was dropped there; the full suite runs the assert live and it never fires.\n\nThis is the second time in this ticket that a justification I wrote was weaker than the checkable fact behind it (the first: calling the index's behaviour 'the silent drop half' when the index was right). Both are now stated as the thing that can be checked.", at: "2026-08-03T11:59:15Z")
